<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    EXACTLY ONE PROJECT REFERENCE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF THIS PROJECT'S GRAPH.

    AAP 0.5.2.2 draws this project with a single edge to Application, and that is what is declared
    below. Domain arrives transitively, so the domain, service, validator, mapper and serialisation
    suites placed here by AAP 0.4.1.1 compile against everything they need.

    WHY THERE IS NO EDGE TO DnnMigration.Infrastructure ANY MORE. There used to be one, kept on the
    reading that AAP 0.5.1.5 names Infrastructure/Security/*.cs as the subject of suites it places
    here, and that dropping the edge would delete coverage. Both halves of that argument were wrong.
    The AAP is frozen and 0.5.2.2 is unambiguous about this project's graph, so an implementation
    that reinterprets it is a divergence rather than a reconciliation; and no coverage had to be
    deleted, because the thirteen suites that named an Infrastructure type were RELOCATED to
    DnnMigration.IntegrationTests - Security/{LoggingAuditSink,AuditLogRendering,
    LegacyCredentialVerifier,JwtTokenServiceBehavior,DatabaseHealthCheck,RefreshTokenStoreBehavior},
    Infrastructure/{StoreFailureClassification,ConnectionStringValidation,DuplicateKeyTranslation,
    DatabaseHealthCheck}, Services/{LoggingAuditSink,ModuleBusinessControllerFactory} and
    Validation/LegacyCredentialOptions - where the Infrastructure assembly is reachable through that
    project's own single edge to the API. Every one of them kept its assertions unchanged and gained
    the [Trait("Category", "Integration")] that project requires.

    WHAT STAYS HERE. Public-contract and shape suites, which is what a project with this graph can
    honestly assert: the Domain entities and their sentinel boundaries, the Application services
    against substituted repositories, the FluentValidation rules, the hand-written mappers, the
    serialisation converters, and the reflected contract shapes of the Domain service abstractions -
    IPasswordHasher, IClock, ISecurityDiagnostics and their kin. Those files name no implementation
    type at all, which is why they compile without the removed edge.

    WHAT THIS PROJECT MUST NOT DO. It must not reimplement a component and then assert that its own
    double behaves as specified; that is a tautology which stays green while the shipped type breaks.
    A suite that needs the real thing resolves it from the composed container in
    DnnMigration.IntegrationTests, through AddInfrastructure, which asserts the registration as well
    as the behaviour.

    Do not add DnnMigration.Infrastructure back, do not ask for an InternalsVisibleTo grant, and do
    not add DnnMigration.Api. The Api layer is the integration project's subject, it is where the
    hosted pipeline and the authorisation policies live, and a unit test that started a host would
    stop being one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
